# Intro
One 29.7 Msun star at the centre of an isolated box (no periodic images) of cold (50 K),
zero-metallicity molecular gas with 8 dense Gaussian clumps (peak density 100 times the clear gas,
clumps 0.12 to 0.32 box sizes from the star). The ISRF transport is on (`ISRF_propagation: 1`) with
the H2 line absorption of the transport on (`ISRF_H2_line_absorption: 1`), so the Lyman-Werner (LW)
light of the star is absorbed by the H2 lines along its path.

At a limited light speed (`ISRF_packet_light_speed: fixed` or `cap`) the light that has not yet
reached a gas particle waits in a reception slot of that particle. A slot is the part of the
particle's reception that holds the light from one direction. When part of the waiting light is
released, the released light receives the mean H2 shielding of the whole waiting pool. This example
runs the cloud twice from the same initial conditions, with a limited and with an unlimited light
speed (no light waits), and checks the first against the second.

The speed of the limited arm is `ISRF_packet_light_speed_fraction_of_c` (0.99 in `params.yml`); the
`fixed` mode uses it directly and `cap` caps the speed at it.

# Configure
This example needs geajay; official Grackle refuses `ISRF_H2_line_absorption: 1` at start-up.
The cooling solver is the geajay default (`GeajayCooling:solver` is not set in `params.yml`).
No extra build flag is needed.
```
./configure --with-chemistry=GEAR_10 --with-feedback=GEAR --with-cooling=geajay_3 --with-stars=GEAR --with-sink=GEAR --with-star-formation=GEAR --with-tracers=GEAR --with-kernel=wendland-C2 --with-geajay=path/to/geajay --with-chealpix=path/to/chealpix
make -j
```
The packet transport needs a radiation table whose `Data/Radiation` group carries
`MeanPhotonEnergyPE` and `Integrated_MeanPhotonEnergyPE`; `run.sh` fetches and checks it.

# Run
```
./run.sh
light_speed=cap ./run.sh      # limited arm with the cap mode instead of the fixed speed
```
`run.sh` fetches the glass file and the tables, writes the initial conditions, runs the unlimited
arm (directory `control`), then the limited arm (directory `limited`), then the check. It deletes
`snap` and `restart` in the current directory first. Both arms write snapshots at the same times.
`level`, `gas_density`, `gas_mass`, `h2_ratio` (initial `nH2/nH`, default 3e-5) and `n_threads`
override the defaults. The run keeps the gas cold and the H2 nearly frozen: photoionization, winds
and supernovae are off, the metallicity is zero, and the time step is set by the light speed
(64 steps of 1.47e-10 internal units). Both arms are non-periodic, so no light of periodic images
of the star reaches the gas.

# Checking
`python3 checkH2WaitingLight.py --limited limited --control control`

Words used by the check:
- share: the light that one neighbour, closer to the star, hands to a particle. It carries the
  neighbour's H2 column and transmission plus the hop between the two.
- pool: the shares that wait together in the slots of one particle.
- hull: the range from the smallest to the largest share value of a particle.

A particle releases a weighted mean of its pool, so its transmission and column must lie inside
the hull of its shares. A share has the H2 column carried by its sender plus the column of the hop,
and the transmission carried by its sender times `f(N + dN) / f(N)`, with `f` the shielding function
at the temperature and density of the receiver, `N` the column of the sender and `dN` the column of
the hop. The hop uses the H2 of the snapshot before the one that holds the sender value. The shares
are built from the carried values of the senders of the same run in all snapshots up to the compared
one, because a share may have waited a few steps.

The check reads every snapshot that both runs hold, from the first at which at least 200 particles
are lit in both runs. Particles are read between 2 smoothing lengths and half a box minus 2
smoothing lengths from the star, and only if their H2 density changed by at most 5 per cent since
the first snapshot. Per snapshot it tests that:
- the limited run lies inside the hull of its own shares (transmission and column);
- the two runs differ by at most the width of the hull of both runs' shares, and both lie inside it;
- the control run lies inside the hull of its own shares.
The comparison ends at the first snapshot where the control leaves its hull, or where the H2 of more
than half the compared particles changed. The check prints the first and last snapshot read and why
the window ends. The two snapshots compared must have the same time, the limited run must have lit
fewer particles than the control at the first compared snapshot (else no light waits), and both runs
must be non-periodic.

What a PASS proves: with waiting light, each particle agrees with the shares of its own senders, and
the two runs agree inside that range. It catches a particle whose transmission or column departs from
what its senders hand to it by more than 1 per cent. What it does not prove: the absolute value of
the transmission. A change that acts on every particle alike moves senders and receivers together
and passes. It does not prove that the released light has the exact value of a per-share sum, nor
that a star on coarse steps is handled correctly. It does not compare a column with the straight
line from the star, so it cannot tell whether the `cap` front is older than light at the speed limit.

The snapshots hold the mean of the slot columns of a particle, not the column of the slot that a share
leaves from, and no slot origin. The check cannot bound that difference. The H2 of a hop is known
only at snapshot times, so the send time is placed within one snapshot interval. A value that sits on
the edge of its hull because it has a single share can therefore lie outside the hull by the size of
these two terms, and the check then fails.

The exit class is 0 for a pass, 1 for a failed bar, 2 for a missing file or field (run `./run.sh`
first), 3 for a non-finite value and 4 for a void run (a premise or the positive control is not met).
